For every distinct value in the shorter array, binary search can check whether it appears in the other sorted array. Using the shorter array for the outer loop keeps the number of searches smaller.

A duplicate value is ignored by checking the last result value. Empty arrays also work because the loops simply do not run.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public int[] intersectionArray(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {
        int[] first = nums1;
        int[] second = nums2;

        if (first.length > second.length) {
            first = nums2;
            second = nums1;
        }

        List<Integer> values = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < first.length; i++) {
            if (i > 0 && first[i] == first[i - 1]) {
                continue;
            }

            if (contains(second, first[i])) {
                values.add(first[i]);
            }
        }

        int[] result = new int[values.size()];
        for (int i = 0; i < values.size(); i++) {
            result[i] = values.get(i);
        }
        return result;
    }

    private boolean contains(int[] numbers, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = numbers.length - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (numbers[middle] == target) {
                return true;
            }
            if (numbers[middle] < target) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle - 1;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(s log l)`, where `s` is the length of the shorter array and `l` is the length of the longer array.

Space complexity: `O(k)` for the result.

This version stores the values from the first array in a `HashSet`. While reading the second array, a value is added to the answer only when it is still in the set. Removing it immediately prevents repeated values in the output.

It does not depend on the sorted order, so the same idea would also work for unsorted arrays. The trade-off is that it uses extra memory for the set.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public int[] intersectionArray(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {
        Set<Integer> available = new HashSet<>();
        for (int value : nums1) {
            available.add(value);
        }

        List<Integer> common = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int value : nums2) {
            if (available.remove(value)) {
                common.add(value);
            }
        }

        int[] result = new int[common.size()];
        for (int i = 0; i < common.size(); i++) {
            result[i] = common.get(i);
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n + m)` on average.

Space complexity: `O(n + k)`, including the set and result.

This is the direct version: take each value from the first array and scan the second array until the value is found. A small `lastAdded` check keeps duplicates out of the result.

Dry run for `[1, 2, 2]` and `[2, 2, 4]`: the first `2` is found and added, while the second `2` is found but skipped because it is already the last result value. If either input is empty, the returned slice is empty.

```go
func IntersectionArray(nums1 []int, nums2 []int) []int {
    result := make([]int, 0)

    for _, value := range nums1 {
        if len(result) > 0 && result[len(result)-1] == value {
            continue
        }

        for _, other := range nums2 {
            if value == other {
                result = append(result, value)
                break
            }
        }
    }

    return result
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n * m)` in the worst case.

Space complexity: `O(k)` for the returned slice.

The arrays are already sorted, so there is no need to sort or search from the beginning each time. I keep one pointer on each array. When the values match, I add the value once and move both pointers. Otherwise, I move the pointer with the smaller value.

Duplicates are skipped by comparing with the last value added. This code does not change either input array.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> intersectionArray(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {
        vector<int> result;
        int i = 0;
        int j = 0;

        while (i < static_cast<int>(nums1.size()) &&
               j < static_cast<int>(nums2.size())) {
            if (nums1[i] == nums2[j]) {
                if (result.empty() || result.back() != nums1[i]) {
                    result.push_back(nums1[i]);
                }
                ++i;
                ++j;
            } else if (nums1[i] < nums2[j]) {
                ++i;
            } else {
                ++j;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n + m)`.

Space complexity: `O(k)` for the returned intersection, where `k` is its size.

